1
00:00:00,181 --> 00:00:03,136
Last time, flame colours came from electrons outside

2
00:00:03,156 --> 00:00:03,679
the nucleus.

3
00:00:04,342 --> 00:00:05,729
Now we go inside it.

4
00:00:06,512 --> 00:00:09,378
An ionisation smoke alarm contains a tiny amount

5
00:00:09,419 --> 00:00:11,243
of americium-two-forty-one,

6
00:00:11,765 --> 00:00:14,531
a radioactive isotope made by people in nuclear

7
00:00:14,572 --> 00:00:15,012
reactors.

8
00:00:15,735 --> 00:00:18,382
The wood of a gum tree contains carbon-fourteen,

9
00:00:18,784 --> 00:00:21,592
which is radioactive too, and completely natural.

10
00:00:22,316 --> 00:00:25,853
So is human-made radiation a different, stronger kind?

11
00:00:26,698 --> 00:00:29,522
Let's find out what radiation unstable isotopes give

12
00:00:29,583 --> 00:00:31,466
off, and whether it matters where they come

13
00:00:31,507 --> 00:00:31,646
from.

14
00:00:32,395 --> 00:00:34,982
Remember from our nuclide video, a nucleus is

15
00:00:35,124 --> 00:00:37,209
unstable when it has the wrong balance of

16
00:00:37,310 --> 00:00:40,018
neutrons and protons, or is simply too big.

17
00:00:40,721 --> 00:00:43,186
An isotope with an unstable nucleus is called

18
00:00:43,207 --> 00:00:44,329
a radioisotope.

19
00:00:45,033 --> 00:00:46,980
Sooner or later its nucleus decays.

20
00:00:47,483 --> 00:00:49,749
It changes to become more stable, and gives

21
00:00:49,851 --> 00:00:50,612
out radiation.

22
00:00:51,395 --> 00:00:54,600
The radiation from decaying nuclei is called ionising,

23
00:00:54,902 --> 00:00:56,845
because it can knock electrons off atoms it

24
00:00:56,906 --> 00:00:58,668
hits, turning them into ions.

25
00:00:59,599 --> 00:01:00,729
There are three main types.

26
00:01:01,396 --> 00:01:02,428
The first is alpha.

27
00:01:03,317 --> 00:01:05,481
An alpha particle is two protons and two

28
00:01:05,562 --> 00:01:08,088
neutrons stuck together, the same as a helium

29
00:01:08,109 --> 00:01:08,549
nucleus.

30
00:01:09,192 --> 00:01:11,081
It has a charge of plus two, and

31
00:01:11,102 --> 00:01:12,408
a relative mass of four.

32
00:01:13,253 --> 00:01:15,057
When a nucleus gives out an alpha particle,

33
00:01:15,258 --> 00:01:17,783
it loses two protons, so it becomes a

34
00:01:17,845 --> 00:01:18,485
different element.

35
00:01:19,168 --> 00:01:22,987
Very heavy nuclei, like uranium-two-thirty-eight, often decay this

36
00:01:23,028 --> 00:01:23,208
way.

37
00:01:24,042 --> 00:01:25,289
The second is beta.

38
00:01:25,972 --> 00:01:28,524
A beta particle is a fast electron, but

39
00:01:28,544 --> 00:01:30,332
it doesn't come from the electron shells.

40
00:01:30,794 --> 00:01:33,279
It's made in the nucleus, when a neutron

41
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:35,865
changes into a proton and an electron, and

42
00:01:35,886 --> 00:01:36,948
the electron shoots out.

43
00:01:37,710 --> 00:01:39,675
A beta particle has a charge of minus

44
00:01:39,896 --> 00:01:41,299
one and almost no mass.

45
00:01:41,982 --> 00:01:44,068
The nucleus now has one more proton, so

46
00:01:44,109 --> 00:01:45,171
it's a different element again.

47
00:01:45,855 --> 00:01:48,972
Nuclei with too many neutrons, like carbon-fourteen, decay

48
00:01:48,993 --> 00:01:49,274
this way.

49
00:01:50,051 --> 00:01:51,223
The third is gamma.

50
00:01:52,011 --> 00:01:54,104
A gamma ray isn't a particle of matter

51
00:01:54,145 --> 00:01:54,406
at all.

52
00:01:54,809 --> 00:01:56,933
It's a photon, like the photons of light

53
00:01:56,974 --> 00:01:57,955
from our spectra video,

54
00:01:58,518 --> 00:01:59,740
but with far more energy.

55
00:02:00,364 --> 00:02:01,956
It has no charge and no mass.

56
00:02:02,823 --> 00:02:04,608
Giving out a gamma ray doesn't change the

57
00:02:04,649 --> 00:02:05,692
protons or neutrons.

58
00:02:06,094 --> 00:02:07,902
The nucleus just gets rid of extra energy,

59
00:02:08,385 --> 00:02:10,233
often right after an alpha or beta decay.

60
00:02:10,983 --> 00:02:11,567
Quick prediction.

61
00:02:12,313 --> 00:02:14,693
Alpha particles are big and highly charged.

62
00:02:15,237 --> 00:02:16,947
Gamma rays have no charge at all.

63
00:02:17,652 --> 00:02:19,847
Which do you think gets further through solid

64
00:02:19,867 --> 00:02:20,290
material?

65
00:02:20,958 --> 00:02:21,347
Have a think.

66
00:02:25,064 --> 00:02:26,396
Gamma travels much further.

67
00:02:27,163 --> 00:02:28,993
Alpha is stopped by a sheet of paper,

68
00:02:29,234 --> 00:02:30,381
or a few centimetres of air.

69
00:02:31,105 --> 00:02:33,316
Beta gets through paper, but is stopped by

70
00:02:33,336 --> 00:02:34,663
a few millimetres of aluminium.

71
00:02:35,507 --> 00:02:38,394
Gamma passes through both, and needs several centimetres

72
00:02:38,434 --> 00:02:40,279
of lead, or thick concrete, to cut it

73
00:02:40,299 --> 00:02:40,780
down a lot.

74
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:41,913
Why?

75
00:02:42,822 --> 00:02:45,867
Alpha is big, slow and strongly charged, so

76
00:02:45,908 --> 00:02:47,510
it collides with atoms constantly,

77
00:02:48,052 --> 00:02:50,476
ionising them, and loses its energy very quickly.

78
00:02:51,379 --> 00:02:53,824
Gamma has no charge, so it rarely hits

79
00:02:53,885 --> 00:02:54,225
anything,

80
00:02:54,748 --> 00:02:55,870
and travels much further.

81
00:02:56,553 --> 00:02:58,700
The more it ionises, the sooner it stops.

82
00:02:59,829 --> 00:03:02,096
Now let's sort some real radioisotopes.

83
00:03:02,682 --> 00:03:03,513
Natural ones first.

84
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:06,985
Uranium-two-thirty-eight has been in rocks since the Earth

85
00:03:07,066 --> 00:03:08,989
formed, and gives out alpha particles.

86
00:03:09,832 --> 00:03:12,816
Carbon-fourteen is made in the upper atmosphere, when

87
00:03:12,937 --> 00:03:15,781
cosmic rays hit nitrogen, and every living thing

88
00:03:15,822 --> 00:03:16,322
takes some in.

89
00:03:16,987 --> 00:03:18,137
It gives out beta particles.

90
00:03:19,045 --> 00:03:22,372
And potassium-forty is in rocks, soil, food and

91
00:03:22,534 --> 00:03:23,094
even your body.

92
00:03:23,637 --> 00:03:25,691
It gives out beta particles, and some gamma

93
00:03:25,712 --> 00:03:25,892
rays.

94
00:03:27,028 --> 00:03:31,295
Americium-two-forty-one, in ionisation smoke alarms, gives out alpha

95
00:03:31,316 --> 00:03:31,696
particles.

96
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:35,171
Cobalt-sixty is made by adding a neutron to

97
00:03:35,212 --> 00:03:36,256
cobalt-fifty-nine.

98
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:39,307
It gives out beta particles, then gamma rays,

99
00:03:39,669 --> 00:03:41,254
and it's used to treat cancers and to

100
00:03:41,315 --> 00:03:42,558
sterilise medical equipment.

101
00:03:43,622 --> 00:03:47,029
And technetium-ninety-nine M gives out gamma rays, for

102
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:47,710
medical scans.

103
00:03:48,493 --> 00:03:51,318
The M means its nucleus holds extra energy,

104
00:03:51,539 --> 00:03:53,182
which it gives out as a gamma ray.

105
00:03:53,944 --> 00:03:56,088
In Australia, the material it comes from is

106
00:03:56,149 --> 00:03:58,472
made in a research reactor at Lucas Heights

107
00:03:58,534 --> 00:03:58,893
in Sydney.

108
00:03:59,531 --> 00:04:00,403
Look at the two columns.

109
00:04:00,928 --> 00:04:03,575
Both have alpha, beta and gamma emitters.

110
00:04:04,278 --> 00:04:07,565
Human-made radioisotopes don't give out a special, stronger

111
00:04:07,646 --> 00:04:08,427
kind of radiation.

112
00:04:08,929 --> 00:04:10,933
The type of radiation is decided by the

113
00:04:11,014 --> 00:04:14,100
nucleus, by its balance of protons and neutrons

114
00:04:14,322 --> 00:04:16,687
and any extra energy it holds, and not

115
00:04:16,708 --> 00:04:17,488
by where it came from.

116
00:04:18,191 --> 00:04:21,157
In fact, carbon-fourteen made in a reactor is

117
00:04:21,358 --> 00:04:24,524
exactly the same as carbon-fourteen made by cosmic

118
00:04:24,605 --> 00:04:27,110
rays, and gives out exactly the same beta

119
00:04:27,151 --> 00:04:27,531
particles.

120
00:04:28,409 --> 00:04:30,516
What does differ is where they come from.

121
00:04:31,219 --> 00:04:34,525
Natural radioisotopes are either very long-lived, left over

122
00:04:34,566 --> 00:04:36,949
since the Earth formed, or constantly made in

123
00:04:37,031 --> 00:04:37,330
nature.

124
00:04:38,053 --> 00:04:40,460
Human-made ones can be made to order, so

125
00:04:40,501 --> 00:04:43,129
scientists can choose the radiation and the half-life

126
00:04:43,231 --> 00:04:43,932
a job needs.

127
00:04:44,796 --> 00:04:46,943
Half-life is the time it takes for half

128
00:04:47,045 --> 00:04:49,212
of the unstable nuclei in a sample to

129
00:04:49,253 --> 00:04:49,593
decay.

130
00:04:50,477 --> 00:04:53,278
Technetium-ninety-nine M's gamma rays pass out of the

131
00:04:53,299 --> 00:04:55,599
body to a camera, and its half-life of

132
00:04:55,660 --> 00:04:58,541
about six hours means its activity falls quickly.

133
00:04:59,363 --> 00:05:02,674
Americium's alpha particles ionise the air inside the

134
00:05:02,715 --> 00:05:04,982
alarm, and travel only a few centimetres.

135
00:05:05,836 --> 00:05:07,143
So here's the pattern to keep.

136
00:05:07,647 --> 00:05:11,312
Paper stops alpha, aluminium stops beta, and lead

137
00:05:11,433 --> 00:05:12,253
cuts down gamma.

138
00:05:12,736 --> 00:05:14,987
The more it ionises, the sooner it stops.

139
00:05:17,703 --> 00:05:18,048
Your turn.

140
00:05:18,941 --> 00:05:22,645
Cobalt-sixty is a human-made radioisotope that gives out

141
00:05:22,725 --> 00:05:24,427
beta particles and gamma rays.

142
00:05:25,048 --> 00:05:28,212
Compare beta particles and gamma rays in terms

143
00:05:28,252 --> 00:05:32,037
of their charge, penetrating ability and ionising ability.

144
00:05:32,781 --> 00:05:33,611
Pause here to answer.

145
00:05:44,929 --> 00:05:46,919
Beta particles have a charge of minus one,

146
00:05:47,342 --> 00:05:48,809
and gamma rays have no charge.

147
00:05:49,572 --> 00:05:51,312
Beta is stopped by a few millimetres of

148
00:05:51,373 --> 00:05:54,456
aluminium, while gamma needs several centimetres of lead

149
00:05:54,477 --> 00:05:55,076
to cut it down.

150
00:05:55,899 --> 00:05:57,687
Beta is more ionising than gamma.

151
00:05:58,451 --> 00:06:00,537
Saying gamma is the most ionising, because it

152
00:06:00,579 --> 00:06:02,263
gets furthest, is the slip.

153
00:06:02,587 --> 00:06:03,339
It's the other way round.

154
00:06:05,091 --> 00:06:05,514
Last one.

155
00:06:06,099 --> 00:06:09,487
A student claims that human-made radioisotopes give off

156
00:06:09,588 --> 00:06:12,494
more dangerous kinds of radiation than natural ones.

157
00:06:13,117 --> 00:06:15,780
Evaluate this claim, using one natural and one

158
00:06:15,861 --> 00:06:17,923
human-made radioisotope as examples.

159
00:06:18,547 --> 00:06:19,321
Pause here to answer.

160
00:06:29,976 --> 00:06:31,463
The claim isn't supported.

161
00:06:32,026 --> 00:06:36,973
Natural uranium-two-thirty-eight and human-made americium-two-forty-one both

162
00:06:37,034 --> 00:06:38,215
give out alpha particles.

163
00:06:38,877 --> 00:06:41,163
The type of radiation depends on the nucleus,

164
00:06:41,505 --> 00:06:42,427
not its origin.

165
00:06:42,990 --> 00:06:45,016
How dangerous a source is depends on its

166
00:06:45,077 --> 00:06:48,328
type, amount, half-life and how you're exposed.

167
00:06:49,131 --> 00:06:52,001
Saying human-made means more dangerous is the trap.

168
00:06:53,857 --> 00:06:54,340
One last thing.

169
00:06:55,085 --> 00:06:57,413
Alpha and beta decay change the number of

170
00:06:57,515 --> 00:07:00,445
protons, so the nucleus becomes a different element.

171
00:07:01,088 --> 00:07:03,962
When uranium-two-thirty-eight gives out an alpha particle,

172
00:07:04,466 --> 00:07:05,289
what's left behind?

173
00:07:05,895 --> 00:07:07,327
And how do the numbers add up?

174
00:07:08,054 --> 00:07:09,819
Next time, we'll learn how to balance a

175
00:07:10,001 --> 00:07:10,802
nuclear reaction.

176
00:07:13,661 --> 00:07:14,484
Here are the key notes.

177
00:07:15,007 --> 00:07:16,555
Pause the video if you need more time

178
00:07:16,595 --> 00:07:17,017
to copy them.
